<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bodhi tree</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bo tree, Sri Maha Bodhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bodhi tree is the sacred fig (Ficus religiosa) at the Mahabodhi Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. According to Buddhist tradition, Siddhartha Gautama attained enlightenment beneath a Bo tree at this site and became the Buddha, around the 5th century BCE. The original tree no longer survives. Recorded episodes of destruction include its felling in the reign of Aśoka (c. 268–232 BCE) and its cutting down by King Śaśāṅka of Gauda around 600 CE, and each time it was replanted from saplings. A sapling taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> survives as the Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi, and the present tree at Bodh Gaya was planted in 1881 from a cutting of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree.jpg" alt="Bodhi tree" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ken Wieland from Philadelphia, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodhgaya_3639641913_f4c5f73689_t.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indonesian Buddhists have hailed its rediscovery and reconstruction as a sign of the Buddhist revival in their country: in 1934 Narada Thera, a missionary monk from Sri Lanka, blessed the planting of a <b>Bodhi tree</b> there, and some upasakas were ordained as monks.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>His presence is shown by his path on the water, a chankrama or &quot;promenade&quot; slab hanging in the air, an empty throne, a footprint, a horse or a canopy under the <b>bodhi tree</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/sanchi">Sanchi · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 3rd century BCE, under Devanampiya Tissa, the monk Mahinda, son of the Maurya emperor Ashoka, brought Buddhism, and a sapling of the <b>Bodhi tree</b> was planted in the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhist sources, including Xuanzang, portray him as a persecutor who cut down the <b>Bodhi tree</b> at Bodh Gaya, though this is probably exaggerated.</blockquote><a href="/state/gauda-kingdom">Gauda Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
